  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA convective line crossing north of Pittsburgh, the remnants of\nearlier stout storms, are producing mainly brief heavy rain and\nwind gusts of up to 40 MPH as they cross. These, as well as\nweaker activity to the south, should exit to the east by\nsunrise.\n\nAfter a brief break later this morning, additional convection is\nlikely to arrive by midday/early afternoon, likely the\ncontinuation of activity currently near the southern end of \nLake Michigan. Another round is then possible after sunset with \na shortwave passage. While exact timing and evolution of \nafternoon/evening convection remains in question, there is still\na decent level of confidence in the presence of an environment \nconducive for severe storms. Model soundings suggest mean-layer \nCAPE values will be in the 1500 to 2000 J/kg range during the \npeak heating period, with deep-layer shear on the order of 25 to\n35 knots. This will support organized convection with mainly a \ndamaging wind threat, both from cold pool consolidation and \ndownburst potential (DCAPE of around 1000 J/kg). Some at least \ntransient supercell structures/rotating storms are also \npossible, thus the threat for a tornado or two cannot be \nignored.\n\nFor Tuesday (and potentially Wednesday as well), the main threat\ncorridor shifts south a bit, with the best potential for severe\nstorms mainly south of I-70 (matching SPC's day 2 Slight Risk \narea). This is where richer instability will reside during the \nafternoon and evening. Again, damaging winds appear to be the \nprimary threat, as this may be the more favored region for \nconvective complex passage. The more concentrated wind damage \nthreat area likely remains just to the south of our region, but \ncould clip portions of at least eastern Ohio.\n\nIn addition to severe threats, there remains the risk of flash\nflooding. Given precipitable water values lifting back into the\n1.6 to 1.7 inch range (90th to 95th percentile in the \nclimatology), as well as the potential for multiple thunderstorm\ncomplexes, WPC continues to highlight our region in a Slight \n(level 2 of 4) Risk for excessive rain for today into Tuesday \nnight, with the main heavy rain threat area shifting south of \nthe Mason-Dixon Line by Wednesday. 48-hour NBM mean \nprecipitation totals are in the 1.5 to 2.5 inch range generally \nsouth of US-422, with 90th percentile totals in the same area of\n3 to 4 inches. Given the still relatively moist soil across the\nregion, such rainfall totals could create at least isolated \nflash flooding issues in the heavier convection. Still, with the\nuncertainty in timing and location of the greatest potential, \nwill hold off on the issuance of a Flood Watch at this time.\n\nBy Thursday, both heavy rain and severe weather threats should \nbegin to subside as a stronger Great Lakes shortwave pushes a\nboundary across the area, leading to less threatening weather.",
